  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery shows a robust shortwave trough \npivoting across the Midwest and western Great Lakes. Associated \nwith this trough is an area of sprinkles and light rain showers \nin eastern IA that is moving towards northern IL. While the dry \nlow-level air across northern IL and northwest IN should limit \nany rain accumulation, some sprinkles are likely to be seen \nthrough 8-9 AM CST this morning as this shortwave and band of \nshowers/sprinkles moves through. Otherwise, expect dry \nconditions for our Saturday with mostly sunny skies and above \naverage temperatures in the lower 50s. \n\nHeading into tonight, another shortwave trough (currently over \nthe Pacific Northwest) will be ejecting across the central \nPlains and begin to pivot into the Ohio River Valley on Sunday. \nAs the shortwave ejects into the Plains this afternoon it is \nforecast to deepen and lead to the development of a sub-1000 mb \nsurface low over the southern Plains, which too will lift into \nthe Ohio River Valley ahead of the shortwave on Sunday. A band \nof rain will develop with this surface low and spread into \nnorthern IL and eventually northwest IN after midnight tonight \nand then persist through Sunday evening before exiting the area.\nWhile our area will be on the northern periphery of the surface\nlow, temperature profiles in the lowest 5-7 kft of the \natmosphere should be above freezing and keep rain as the \ndominant precipitation type with this system. So expect a \ngenerally un-impactful soaking rainfall with rain amounts around\n0.5 to 1.0 inch, though some locally higher rain totals are \npossible in north-central IL closer to the surface low center \nand the mid-level baroclinic zone. \n\nIn the wake of the rain, high pressure will move back into the \nregion and persist through most of the upcoming week. Winds \nbehind the low will be out of the northwest and on the breezy \nside with gusts around 20-30 mph late Sunday afternoon into \nMonday morning, but winds will ease and turn southerly by Monday\nevening. As a result, temperatures will be near seasonable with\nreadings in the 40s for Sunday and Monday but will rebound back \nto above normal with highs in the 50s by Tuesday. The next \nchance for any measurable precipitation is not expected until \nthe end of next week when another storm system is forecast to \nmove through the Midwest and Great Lakes.\n\nYack",
